\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
SentimenAI:
 Implementation of Naive Bayes and Support Vector Machine Methods for E-Commerce Product Review Sentiment Classification Based on Hugging Face Datasets with FastAPI REST API Integration and Next.js Dashboard
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
This research is motivated by the inconsistency of prior studies in comparing the performance of Naive Bayes and Support Vector Machine (SVM) algorithms on Indonesian-language review data,
 which calls for a renewed empirical evaluation on a standardized dataset.
 This study aims to implement and compare both methods,
 build a FastAPI-based REST API,
 develop an interactive Next.js dashboard equipped with a Large Language Model-based Generate Insight feature,
 and provide SQLite-based analysis history storage,
 so that the algorithm validation results do not merely remain as figures in a research report but are realized as a genuinely usable system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
The research method employed the ADDIE development model,
 using the sepidmnorozy/Indonesian_sentiment dataset from Hugging Face Datasets (10,192 data points),
 a four-stage preprocessing pipeline (case folding,
 character removal,
 stopword removal,
 and stemming using PySastrawi),
 TF-IDF feature extraction (1-2 ngram range,
 10,000 features),
 and training of Multinomial Naive Bayes and LinearSVC models.
 System testing was conducted in multiple layers,
 including black box testing,
 white box testing,
 boundary testing,
 5-fold cross validation,
 hyperparameter tuning using GridSearchCV,
 preprocessing ablation,
 error analysis,
 API response time measurement,
 and heuristic evaluation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
The results show that SVM outperformed Naive Bayes with an accuracy of 88.44% (precision 88.35%,
 recall 88.44%,
 F1-score 88.36%) compared to 87.56% for Naive Bayes,
 remaining consistent under 5-fold cross validation (standard deviation ±0.29%).
 Hyperparameter tuning improved SVM accuracy to 89.19%.
 The ablation study found that stopword removal decreased accuracy by up to 3.36 percentage points because it also removed negation words.
 The system was successfully implemented with twelve REST API endpoints and a median response time of 6.51 milliseconds,
 along with a dashboard featuring four functional tabs and a Generate Insight page.
\end_layout

